#v(0.8em)

The second couple in a family are

- Parents.
- Children.

In laying down their duties, the Apostle begineth with children: his direction, and instigation unto them is laid down in the three first verses of the sixth chapter: wherein

- 1\. He declareth their #emph[duty.]
- 2\. He addeth #emph[reasons] to enforce the same.

In laying down their duty he noteth three points.

- 1\. #emph[Wherein] it consisteth, #emph[(obey, honour.)]
- 2\. #emph[To whom] it is to be performed #emph[(your parents.)]
- 3\. #emph[After what manner] it is to be done #emph[(in the Lord.)]

The reasons used by the Apostle are four.

- 1\. The #emph[place] of parents #emph[(in the Lord.)]
- 2\. The #emph[aequity] of the thing #emph[(this is right.)]
- 3\. The #emph[charge] of God #emph[(honour thy father,] etc.)
- 4\. The #emph[reward] promised #emph[(That it may go well,] etc.)

Under this word #emph[(obey)] which the Apostle useth, and that word #emph[(honour)] which the law useth, are all those duties comprised, which any where throughout the whole Scripture are enjoined to children.

We will therefore set them down in some order, and handle them distinctly one after another.

- 1\. The #emph[Fountain] of childrens duties is to be searched out.
- 2\. The #emph[Streams] that flow from thence are to be observed.

The #emph[Fountain] is an inward disposition of the heart compounded of #emph[love] and #emph[fear.]

The #emph[Streams] issuing from thence extend unto parents, both while they are #emph[living,] and also when they are #emph[dead.]

Childrens duties which are to be performed to their parents while they live, have respect to their

+ #emph[Authority.]
+ #emph[Necessity.]

The #emph[Authority] of parents requireth of children

+ #emph[Reverence.]
+ #emph[Obedience.]

Their #emph[Necessity] requireth #emph[Recompense.]

The duties which children owe to their parents deceased, respect their

+ #emph[Body.]
+ #emph[Credit.]

Their #emph[Body] with decency must be #emph[buried.]

Their #emph[Credit] with honour must be #emph[maintained.]

=== §. 2. #emph[Of Childrens love to their Parents.]

I make the fountain of childrens duties to be a mixed and compound disposition, in respect of that #emph[authority and affection] which is mixed together in parents. The #emph[authority] of parents requireth #emph[fear] in children: and their #emph[affection, love.] So entire and so ardent is parents #emph[affection] towards their children, as it would make children too bold and insolent if there were not #emph[authority] mixed therewith to work #emph[fear:] and so supreme and absolute is their #emph[authority] over them, as it would make children like slaves to dread their parents, if a fatherly #emph[affection] were not tempered therewith to breed #emph[love.] But both these joined together make a very good composition: #emph[love] like sugar sweetneth #emph[fear,] and #emph[fear] like salt seasoneth #emph[love:] and thus, to join them both together, it is a #emph[loving-fear,] or a #emph[fearinglove,] which is the ground of childrens duties.

Where Christ forbiddeth an excessive love in children to #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 10. 37.] their parents, he implieth that parents are a fit object for children to love (so as their love be well moderated:) yea he implieth that it is an affection even by nature ingraffed in children to love their parents. #emph[Joseph] is commended unto children as a worthy pattern in loving his father, and that from his youth till the decease of his father: in testimony whereof in his younger years he brought to his father the #emph[evil report of his] #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 37. 2.] #emph[brethren,] whereby he incurred their envy and hatred, which he would never have done, if he had not loved his father: and having been long absent from his father, when by Gods providence there was offered an occasion for him to meet with his brethren, one of his first questions to them was about their father: #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 43. 7.] and hearing that he was living, he thought it not enough to send him food for his need, but must also needs see his face, #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 45. 9.] and have him dwell with him: and while his father was in the way he went out to meet him, and at first sight fell on his neck, #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 46. 29.] and wept a good while (a token of great affection.)

That love which naturally parents bear to their children, #footnote[Reasons. 1. Equity.] ought in equity to breed in children a love to their parents. For love deserueth love: and most unworthy are they to be loved, who cannot love again. The love of parents above all others is to be answered with love on childrens part to the uttermost of their power, because it is free, great, and constant.

Besides, there is a necessity of love in children to their parents, #footnote[2 Necessity.] lest for want thereof, their subjection (which of all others ought to be most free) should turn into slavish servitude.

This ought children the rather to labour after, because by nature they are nothing so prone to love their parents, as their parents are to love them. Love is weighty, and, as weighty things, it descendeth. Children therefore with conscience of duty must labour to make supply of this defect, and help nature by grace. I deny not but naturally there is in children a greater love to their parents, then to others: yet in comparison of the heat of parents love to them, their love to their parents is but cold. Wherefore as the heat of the Sun shining much and long on a stone wall, draweth a reflection of heat from that wall: so the hot beams of parents love, which with fervency and constancy is cast on children, ought to provoke and stir up children to send forth a reflection of love on their parents.

Two extremes are contrary to this affection of love.

One is #emph[want of natural affection,] which is a vice most odious #footnote[#emph[Rom.] 1. 30. 2 #emph[Tim.] 3. 3.] and abominable in all, but most of all in children. The Apostle reckoneth this among the most heighnous vices that be.

The other is hatred and despight of parents: a vice more then monstrous, and unnatural. From thence cometh mocking and cursing of parents, whereof we shall afterwards hear.

=== §. 3. #emph[Of a childes fear of his parent.]

To the forenamed duty of love, must #emph[fear] be added, which is a childes #emph[awful respect of his parent.]

This #emph[awful respect] ariseth from an honourable esteem which a child in his judgment and opinion hath of his parent, as he is his parent; and from it proceedeth on the one side, a desire and indeavour in all things to please the parent, and on the other side a loathness to offend him.

In this respect the fear of a #emph[child] is opposed to the fear of a #emph[slave.] For a childes fear being mixed with love, hath respect to the #emph[offence] which a parent may take; but a slaves fear, which is ordinarily mixed with hatred, hath respect to nothing but the #emph[punishment] which his master may inflict upon him. The forenamed fear is so proper to children, as that awful respect which the Saints bear to God, is called a #footnote[See Treat. 1. §. 4.] #emph[siliall] or #emph[child-like fear.]

This #emph[fear] in a child is an especial branch of that #footnote[#emph[Exo.] 20. 12.] #emph[honour] which the law requireth of children to their parents: and it is in #footnote[#emph[Leu.] 19. 3.] express terms enjoined to children by the law. That phrase which God useth of #emph[Miriam] (#footnote[#emph[Num.] 12. 14.] #emph[If her father had but spit in her face, should she not be ashamed seven days?)] showeth that there ought to be such a fear of the parent in a childes heart, as should work shame in it when the parent is offended.

A worthy pattern we have hereof in #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 27. 12.] #emph[Jacob,] who was loath to gain the blessing with offence of his father.

This #emph[fear] keepeth #emph[love] in compass: and restraineth a child #footnote[#emph[Reasons.] 1. Fear ordereth love.] from overmuch sauciness, and malipartness.

And it is a cause of a childes reverend and dutiful carriage #footnote[2\. It well guideth the carriage.] to his parent. For as the heart is affected the carriage will be ordered.

Contrary hereunto is that #emph[light,] or (which is more abominable) #footnote[Contrary, light and base esteem of parents.] that #emph[base] and #emph[vile] esteem of parents, which is in the heart of many children: especially if parents be poor, of low degree, unlearned, ignorant, or subject to any infirmities. It cannot be but that #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 9. 22.] #emph[Cham] had too light, if not a base esteem of his father, when he derided him. A true filial fear would have restrained him from that extreme.

Wherefore to breed and cherish this fear, and to prevent, #footnote[How to breed and cherish a filial fear.] or redress the contrary extreme, let children well inform themselves of their parents place and authority, how they are in Gods stead, and a means under God of their childrens being: children have received their very substance from the substance of their parents. In which respect though they should seem contemptible to others, yet not to their children.

Thus much of a childes inward disposition towards his parent. The manifestation thereof must be by his outward carriage: and that in two things; #emph[reverence,] and #emph[obedience:] both which respect a parents authority.

=== §. 4. #emph[Of a childes] Reverence #emph[in refraining speech before his parent, and in hearkening to his parent.]

The outward #emph[reverence] which children owe to their parents consisteth partly in their #emph[speech,] partly in their carriage.

Their speech both #emph[to] and #emph[of] their parents must savour reverence.

To their parents in presence.

OF their parents in absence.

In presence, by

+ #emph[Refraining]
+ #emph[Well framing]

their speech.

For refraining speech two virtues are requisite

+ #emph[Silence.]
+ #emph[Patience.]

Silence in

+ #emph[Forbearing] to speak.
+ #emph[Breaking] of speech. #footnote[Children ought to forbear speaking in their parents presence]

Patience in hearkening to their parents.

The two branches of silence, in #emph[forbearing] to speak (especially when parents are speaking, or till parents give leave to their children to speak,) and in breaking off speech, when parents come into the place where children are speaking, are tokens of great reverence. Thus children testify that there are some in place whom they much respect and honour. #emph[Job] doth thus set forth the respect which Princes and others did bear to him in his prosperity, #emph[The Princes] (saith he) #emph[refrained] #footnote[#emph[Job.] 29. 9, 10.] #emph[talking, and laid their hand upon their mouth, the Nobles held their peace, etc.] Namely, while he was in presence, or while he spake. The like may be said of childrens patience in induring #footnote[Children must patiently hearken to their parents speech. #emph[Vers.] 25.] their parents speech; which #emph[Job] also noteth in these words, #emph[Unto me men gave ear, and waited, and kept silence.] Though parents in their speech seem to be long and tedious, yet must children indure it.

And it is very needful that patience be added to silence, because many parents in tender love of their children, and earnest desire of their good, think they can never speak enough in instructing and admonishing them. The many exhortations #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 49. 2. #emph[Pro.] 1. 8, #emph[&] 4. 1. #emph[&] 7. 1.] given in Scripture unto children to #emph[hear, hearken, give ear, give heed, mark, and observe] the words of their parents, do imply the forenamed #emph[silence] and #emph[patience:] for they who #footnote[#emph[Iam] 1. 19.] ought to be #emph[swift to hear] must be #emph[slow to speak.] I deny not but much more is intended under those phrases, namely, #emph[obedience:] yet must #emph[these] also be presupposed: for he that will not in silence patiently hearken to his parents while they speak, will much less obey what they say.

Contrary to silence is sawciness (as we speak) and overmuch #footnote[Contrary, 1. boldness of speech.] boldness in children, when, without due respect of their parents presence, they will be prating of this thing or that thing: insomuch as if strangers should come into the room where such children are, they would not think that their parents were in presence; or if they knew it, they might well think that such children bear little respect to their parents.

Contrary to patience in hearing, is fretting and murmuring #footnote[2\. Impatient hearkening to parents.] against parents (if at least their speech be any whit long) and flinging or slinking away before they have done. These faults are the greater, if children by their loquacity, or impatiency hinder or interrupt their parents speech when they are giving any admonition or instruction: for thus they show both too light a respect of their parents: and also too little regard of the means of their own good.

=== §. 5. #emph[Of a childes reverend framing his speech to his parent.]

A childes reverence in well framing his speech to his parents #footnote[A childs reverence in speech is manifested, 1. By reverend titles.] may many ways be manifested, as

1. By giving unto them reverend and honourable titles. No title can be more honourable then that which is most proper and usual, #emph[father] to the one parent, and #emph[mother] to the other. God taketh the title #footnote[#emph[Jer.] 31. 9. #emph[Gal.] 4. 6.] #emph[father] unto himself, as a title of great dignity.

#strong[#emph[Object.] This title is so proper to God, as #footnote[#emph[Matt.] 23. 9.] #emph[We are to call none in earth Father.]]

#emph[Answ.] This is not simply to be taken of the title it self, but of the mind of him that giveth or affecteth that title.

If it be affected or given to obscure Gods #emph[Fatherhood,] or to make a man #emph[a father] of himself without dependance on God, or reference to him, who is properly the father of all, it is an impious and sacrilegious title. But otherwise lawful and warrantable.

In Scripture the title #emph[father] is given to all degrees of dignities among men, as to #footnote[1 #emph[Sam.] 24. 12.] #emph[Kings,] #footnote[2 #emph[Kin.] 5. 13.] #emph[Captains,] and other #emph[chief Governors,] to #footnote[#emph[Judg.] 18. 19.] #emph[Priests,] #footnote[2 #emph[King.] 6. 21.] #emph[Prophets,] #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 4. 15.] #emph[Apostles,] and other #footnote[1 #emph[Tim.] 5. 1.] #emph[Ministers.] In the fifth commandment all superiors are comprised under it, therefore #emph[father] is a title of great honour: and by the rule of relation #emph[mother] is a title of as great honour to the female sex. Religious and dutiful children have ever used to give those titles to their parents. #emph[My father,] saith #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 22. 7.] #emph[Isaac] to #emph[Abraham,] and #footnote[#emph[&] 27. 18.] #emph[Jacob] to #emph[Isaac.] #footnote[1 #emph[King.] 2. 20.] #emph[My mother,] saith #emph[Solomon] to #emph[Bathsheba.] I find also the title of #footnote[#emph[Matt.] 21. 30.] #emph[Sir] or #emph[Lord,] used: a title #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 31. 35.] of honour.

2. By using #emph[few words] before their parents: and those few #footnote[2\. By few words.] not without just occasion, being first spoken to by their parents, or having leave of them, or making known to them me needful matter: at least not against their parents liking, as their parents should be offended thereby. And if they observe their parents to be unwilling to hear them speak any more of such and such a matter, then ought they to lay their hands upon their mouths, as #emph[Isaac (Gen.] 22. 7.) and #emph[Jacob (Gen.] 27. 12.) This is a token of great respect.

3. By meek and humble speeches. Such was the speech of #footnote[3\. By humble speech.] #footnote[1 #emph[Sam.] 19 4.] #emph[Jonathan] the natural son of #emph[Saul,] and of #footnote[#emph[&] 24. 10.] #emph[David] his son in law, wherewith he was much contented, and his wrath pacified.

4. By observing a fit opportunity: as when parents are not #footnote[4 By seasonable speech.] seriously busied, or in company, or in passion. When #footnote[1 #emph[Sam.] 19. 6.] #emph[Saul] was out of passion, how well did he accept #emph[Jonathans] Apology for #emph[David?] but #footnote[#emph[&] 20. 30.] in his passion, how ill did he take it? This wise observing of fit opportunity showeth great reverence.

5. By a present, ready, willing, pleasing answer, when by #footnote[5\. By ready answers.] their parents they shall be spoken unto. #emph[Eli] was as a father to #emph[Samuel:] therefore when #footnote[1 #emph[Sam.] 3. 4, 6,] #emph[Samuel] thought that #emph[Eli] called him, he presently and readily answered, #emph[Here am I:] and when #emph[Eli] was instant to know what the Lord had said to him, #footnote[18.] #emph[Samuel told him every whit, and held nothing from him.] #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 21. 30.] The younger son (noted in the parable) showed a son-like reverence in giving a willing and ready answer to his father, though he failed in his obedience, by not performing what he promised.

=== §. 6. #emph[Of the vices in children contrary to the forenamed reverence in speech.]

Contrary to those branches of reverence in speech are, #footnote[Contrary, 1. Scorning to acknowledge a parent.]

1. #emph[Pride:] when children scorn to give the title of #emph[father,] or #emph[mother,] to their parents. This is the mind of many who have gotten more wealth or honour, then ever their parents had. In public especially such children most refuse to give those titles. #emph[Solomon] was not so minded. He being a great king, sitting upon his throne, in sight and hearing of all his people that were about him, called #emph[Bathsheba mother.] #footnote[1 #emph[King.] 2. 20.]

If children had that regard to the honour of their parents which they should, they being themselves in places of honour and dignity, would the rather openly call their parents #emph[father] and #emph[mother,] that they might be known to be the father and mother of so eminent a person.

2. #emph[Loquacity,] and too much importunity, or rather impudency #footnote[2\. Too much importunity.] in speech, when children having to do with their parents, can never have done (as we speak) but must needs use matters to the very uttermost. Many parents are oft much provoked hereby. It skilleth not that the child have the right, especially in a matter of no great consequence. For reverence sake the child must forbear, at least for a time. And if the matter of difference be weighty, as in points of religion, the child must either take some other opportunity of better informing his parent, or else get some other wise friend to do it.

3. #emph[Stoutness,] when children answer their parents as if they #footnote[3\. Stout answers. #emph[Ne filius cum patre quoquo modo expostulet, cum lex interdixit, tum anteleges ipsa natura. Greg. Naz. in senten. Matt.] 21. 29. #emph[Luke] 15. 29.] were their equals: giving word for word. It doth as ill become children to #emph[answer again,] as servants (to whom the Apostle hath expressly forbidden it, #emph[Tit.] 2. 9.) Both law and nature forbiddeth children to be provoked hereunto, by any thing that their parents say or do; how great then is their fault who give scornful and stout words to their parents when they are no way provoked, as the #emph[elder son] noted in the parable, and the #emph[elder brother] of the prodigal child?

4. #emph[Indiscretion,] when children have no respect to any time, business, or temper, of their parents in speaking to them, and #footnote[4\. Unseasonable speech. #emph[Ephes.] 6. 4.] so, much provoke them. It is laid down as a caveat to parents, that they #emph[provoke not their children to wrath.] How much more must children observe that cavear?

5. #emph[Stubborness,] when children pout, lour, swell, and give #footnote[5\. Stomachfull silence.] to answer at all to their parents. This is too common a fault in children, and many parents are much offended and grieved thereat. We heard #footnote[§. 4.] before of a childelike silence which was very commendable, and a token of great reverence; but this is worthy of much blame, a token of great undutifulness; and carefully to be avoided, as that to be practised.

=== §. 7. #emph[Of childrens reverend speeches of their parents.]

So true and entire ought that reverend respect to be which children bear to their parents, as their speech not only #emph[to them] before their faces, but also #emph[of them] behind their backs, must be so framed both for matter and manner, when they have any occasion to fall into speech of their parents, as all that hear them may note them to bear a revererend respect to their parents. #footnote[Direction for children how to speak of their parents.]

As a general direction for the better performing of this duty, let children speak nothing of their parents that they would be loath should come to their parents ear. More particularly, let them speak of those things which most tend to their commendation, that so (as Christ said of his father #footnote[#emph[John] 8. 49.]) they may honour their parents. Let other things be buried in silence so much as in them lieth. And if others speak of matters disgraceful to their parents, let them interpret in the better sense things doubtful, and, so far as they may, extenuate things evident, and sharply reprove them that slander their parents. This is that #emph[blessing] which children owe to their parents, for neglecting whereof the wiseman taxeth children saying, #emph[There is a generation that doth not bless their] #footnote[#emph[Prov.] 30. 11.] #emph[mother.]

Contrary to that kind of blessing is discovering of parents #footnote[Childrens unreverend speeches of their parents.] infirmities, noted in cursed #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 9. 22.] #emph[Cham,] and broaching untruths of them, noted in impious #footnote[2 #emph[Sam.] 15. 3.] #emph[Absalom,] and mocking and cursing them #footnote[#emph[Prov.] 30. 11.] expressly condemned. The reward whereof is by #footnote[#emph[Leu.] 20. 9.] Gods law death: yea a shameful and ignominious death, for the #footnote[#emph[Pro.] 30. 17.] #emph[Ravens of the valley shall pluck out his eyes, and the young Eagles shall eat it:] #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 40. 19.] which phrase setteth forth the end of a notorious malefactor that is hanged.

=== §. 8. #emph[Of a childs reverend carriage to his parent.]

As the speech, so the carriage of children towards their parents must be seasoned with reverence: for

- 1\. #emph[This] is a fruit, and proof of filial fear as well as #emph[that.]
- 2\. Of the two, this is the surer evidence: for actions are better signs of the disposition of the heart then words.
- 3\. Fair words joined with contrary deeds, cannot but be accounted merely complemental and hypocritical.
- 4\. Where there is a contrariety betwixt words and deeds, the one will be a witness against the other, and that mans condemnation the greater. #footnote[Childrens reverence to their parents manifested, 1. by meeting them.]

Wherefore let all reverence be manifested in childrens behaviour to their parents, and that in these and such like instances.

1. If a parent be coming to a child, and the child observe it, let him haste to meet his parent: so did #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 46. 29.] #emph[Joseph] to his father, and #footnote[1 #emph[King.] 2. 19.] #emph[Solomon] to his mother. Which two examples are the rather to be noted, because both were in eminent place: one a great governor, the other a king.

2. Let such child-like obeisance be performed as becometh #footnote[2\. By obeisance to them.] the age and sex, either in going to, remaining before, or going from a parent: as uncovering the head, bending the knee, bowing the body, standing up, with the like. The two forenamed eminent persons, #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 48. 12.] #emph[Joseph] and #footnote[1 #emph[King.] 2. 19.] #emph[Solomon,] bowed, the one to his father, the other to his mother.

3. Let the countenance, and gesture of the body be so soberly #footnote[3 By well composing the countenance.] and modestly ordered in the presence of the parent, as may argue due respect.

4. Let the upper place, and hand be given to parents: and #footnote[4\. By giving the upper hand.] if occasionally a child be above his parent, let him come below him. For that is a manifest token of inferiority and subjection. What maketh men to strive for the upper hand, but because they would be accounted better then those with whom they strive? But that ought not to be the mind of children to their parents.

#strong[#emph[Quest.] What if children be in estate more wealthy, or honourable then their parents, are they then to give the hand to them?]

#emph[Answ.] No honour is comparable to the dignity of fatherhood: it giveth a greater eminency to the parent over his child, then any other honour can to the child over his parent. I grant that a child may by some office, and outward dignity be so advanced above his father, as other men may more honour and reverence the child, and give the upper place to him: and for order sake the child may and ought to take it in company: but when they are alone, the child must rather reverence the father.

5. According to the custom of the time and place wherein #footnote[5\. By asking blessing.] they live, let children ask their parents blessing.

=== §. 9. #emph[Of childrens asking their parents blessing, whether it be lawful or no.]

Some doubt is made of this duty both in regard of the thing it self, and also of the gesture of kneeling used in the performance thereof: I will therefore distinctly prove both.

For the thing, it is noted of #emph[Jacob] that he carried savory meat to his father, that #emph[he might bless him:] and of #emph[Joseph,] that #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 27. 19.] he went to his father, and carried his two sons with him, #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 48. 1. #emph[etc.]] that his father might #emph[bless] both him and them: for which end the twelve sons of #emph[Jacob] assembled to their father. #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 49. 1. #emph[etc.] How the blessings which the patriarchs gave their children are patterns to others.]

#emph[Object.] These were extraordinary examples: the Patriarchs were indued with the spirit of prophesy, whereby they revealed to their children what their estate should be in the times to come: for knowledge whereof their children came to them.

1. #emph[Answ.] Their blessings were more then predictions of things to come: they were confirmations and assurances to the #footnote[See Treat. 6. §. 58.] children that God would indeed perform that blessing which their parents had pronounced. For they sustained a double person: the person of a Prophet, and of a father; as prophets they foretold things to come: as fathers they obtained the blessings pronounced, and an assurance thereof to their children, #footnote[#emph[Heb.] 11. 20.] and that by faith and prayer.

2. #emph[Answ.] Though all parents cannot with such an extraordinary spirit assure unto their children any distinct particular blessing, yet the faithful prayer of parents is an especial, and ordinary means to obtain a blessing from God upon their children: and that because of Gods promise which extends #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 17. 7. #emph[Act.] 2. 39.] it self not only to fathfull parents, but also to their seed. Wherefore as the children of the patriarchs came to their fathers to be assured of some extraordinary blessing, so may other children go to their parents as a means to obtain an ordinary blessing. It is noted of #emph[Eliah] that by an extraordinary spirit in prayer he obtained extraordinary matters: Yet #footnote[#emph[Iam.] 5. 17.] the Apostle setteth forth that example to all Christians as a motive to stir them up in faith to pray for ordinary blessings. But for further clearing of this point, note the phrase used in the fifth commandment as a reason to move children to honour their parents: this it is word for word, #emph[That they may prolong thy days, etc.] how can parents prolong their childrens days, but by begging that blessing of God? #footnote[See Treat. 6. §. 4. #emph[Prov.] 15. 8. How wicked parents may bless their children.] The prayers then of parents are a great blessing to children, and children ought to seek this blessing of their parents.

#emph[Object.] If parents be wicked, their prayer is abomination: what blessing then can children look for from wicked parents?

#emph[Answ.] Though God hear not wicked parents in love and goodness to themselves, yet for the good of their children he may and will hear them: and that the rather to maintain a reverend respect of parents in the heart of their children. For asking a blessing is an acknowledgment of superiority and authority, according to that of the Apostle, #emph[The less is blessed] #footnote[#emph[Heb.] 7. 7.] #emph[of the greater.]

Concerning the gesture of kneeling, it is answerable to the #footnote[It is lawful for children to kneel to their parents. #emph[Gen.] 48. 12.] gesture which of old was used by Gods people in like case: of #emph[Joseph] it is said that #emph[he bowed down himself with his face to the earth.]

#strong[#emph[Object.] Kneeling is a gesture proper to Gods worship.]

#emph[Answ.] It is not so proper, but that it may be used in civil cases: else Christ would have reproved the young man for #emph[kneeling] before him as well as for calling him #emph[good:] for he conceived #footnote[#emph[Mar.] 10. 17.] Christ to be but a mere man, and the worship he did him was but civil.

It is not simply the gesture, but the occasion of the gesture, the mind of him that performeth it, and the end why he performeth it that maketh it divine, or civil. #emph[Cornelius] fell down before #emph[Peter] with conceit of some divine excellency in him, and was not allowed: his manner of worshiping was divine. The jaylor fell down before #emph[Paul] and #emph[Silas] in acknowledgment of some outward eminency in them, and was not reproved: his manner of worshipping was merely civil. The same gesture may be performed to different persons with a different respect. A child may kneel to his parent, and to the king. Yet it followeth not that he maketh his parent a king. Neither will it follow that by kneeling to his parent he maketh him a God, because men kneel to God.

=== §. 10. #emph[Of the vices contrary to childrens reverend gesture towards their parents.]

Contrary to the forenamed branches of reverend gesture, are, #footnote[Unreverend gestures of children.]

1. #emph[Rudeness] and #emph[unmannerliness,] when children know not how to put difference betwixt their parents and strangers, but can #footnote[1\. Rudeness.] suffer their parents to come to them, and they abide in their place and not stir to meet them.

2. #emph[Disdainful stateliness,] when they think much to stand #footnote[2\. Stateliness.] bare-headed any while in their parents presence. It falleth out many times, that when parents and children are together before their betters, #emph[they] will show more reverence then #emph[these:] for the father will stand, and be uncovered, when the son sitteth down and puts on his hat, upon conceit that his father doth more reverence then is meet: but if it were so, yet the son for the fathers sake should stoop somewhat the lower.

3. #emph[Wantonness] and #emph[boldness,] when children are over-familiar #footnote[3\. Boldness.] with their parents: toying and gigling upon every light occasion. This kind of carriage cannot but much tend to the disgrace and dishonour of parents. For what can they who behold it think, but that such children have been too much cockered and ill nurtered?

4. #emph[Ambition,] when children are so ambitiously desirous of place, #footnote[4\. Ambition.] especially in company, as rather then be under some whom they suppose to be at least their equals, they will be above their parents. This oft falleth out, when parents being of a lowly mind, give place to such as their children, being of a lofty mind, think meaner then themselves. Now rather then they will be under their inferiors (as they suppose) they will be above their parents. A point of great insolency. Such ought to be the respect of a child to his parent, as he should debase himself below those that are his inferiors, rather then exalt himself above his parent. As with other men, for peace sake, in many cases, a man must depart from his right; so especially with his parent, in case of superiority. Would not every one that knows what honour a child owes to a father, condemn that childs ambition, that should so stand upon the place and hand, as to take them of his parent?

5. An #emph[over-nice] and erroneous #emph[opinion] of those, who think it #footnote[5\. Refusing to ask blessing.] unmeet for any child to ask their parents blessing. Their own conceit more swayeth them, then the continuali approved practise of Gods people in all ages: not unlike him whom #emph[Solomon] saith to be #emph[wiser in his own conceit, then seven men that can render] #footnote[#emph[Pro.] 26. 16.] #emph[a reason.] Others, though they do not so generally disallow this duty, yet they think it meet only for young children: not considering of what years, stature, and state, #emph[Joseph] was, when he performed it.

As for those, who think it not unlawful, yet carelessly neglect it, they little consider the benefit of a parents blessing. Profane #emph[Esau] shall another day rise up in judgment against them. He #footnote[#emph[Gen] 27. 34. #emph[Heb.] 12 17.] beggd and beggd again and again, and that with a loud cry and salt tears, a blessing of his father.

Thus much of childrens reverence. Their obedience followeth.
